<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parthenon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">447–432 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parthenon is the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> Parthenos on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens">Acropolis of Athens</a>, built between 447 and 432 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> by the architects <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ictinus">Ictinus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callicrates">Callicrates</a>, with sculpture supervised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phidias">Phidias</a>. It later served as a church, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> cathedral and a mosque. In 1687 a Venetian shell ignited Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> stored inside, destroying much of the building.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthenon.jpg" alt="Parthenon" width="320" height="225" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Steve Swayne · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Parthenon_in_Athens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
